athbf{b}$, then $\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761479 1072 #uuid "08d70796-c997-57db-a8ed-490cb432e061" 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761479 1074 "First trace $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the common origin $O$ to a point $P$: keep its direction and scale its length. The remaining path from $P$ to the endpoint $C$ must be the unknown multiple of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n![](images/a0/a0e3e19b1ca51ba09da116914e7c11bf155f01c2a4360f4956c28280491f9a1b.png)\n\nFollowing the grid direction of $\\mathbf{a}$ takes $2$ steps, while the parallel path $PC$ has $2$ steps. Their length ratio is $\\frac{2}{2}=1$. The remaining path points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$, so\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=-\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\nAdding the path vectors gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}\\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{b}+(-\\mathbf{a})\\\\\n&=-\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBecause $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are nonparallel, these coefficients are unique. Comparing with $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=-1$. The minus sign records the reversed direction." 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761488 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761480 1071 "The three vectors share an origin, and each grid square has the same side length.\n\n![](images/47/472d6130b8d885f7d053919197ccb8a2890ff142afa37c7f1741ae6826966039.png)\n\nGiven $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$." 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761480 1072 #uuid "0905f52b-1aee-5bea-8f19-810a7454deec" 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761480 1074 "First trace $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the common origin $O$ to a point $P$: keep its direction and scale its length. The remaining path from $P$ to the endpoint $C$ must be the unknown multiple of $\\ma