f 17592186045498 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761510 1083 #uuid "d252b653-2700-5f8d-804c-dba0ef752997" 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761511 1080 "-16" 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761511 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761511 1083 #uuid "d4194278-3edd-5061-b5e9-3948c6211a64" 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761508 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761512 1071 "The three vectors share an origin, and each grid square has the same side length.\n\n![](images/74/74e0f3497892aeda037d5aadaa57fdbb1f38d492b14e7977550f14c6330c6822.png)\n\nIf $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$, then $\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761512 1072 #uuid "df910369-e57e-53f2-92c7-597b56fc17b3" 13194139547592 17592186576411 true] [17592186761512 1074 "First trace $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the common origin $O$ to a point $P$: keep its direction and scale its length. The remaining path from $P$ to the endpoint $C$ must be the unknown multiple of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n![](images/7a/7a5036650f84013328da57fbc60eba029b45a30b5d8166dc6c24cfed9e0ff175.png)\n\nFollowing the grid direction of $\\mathbf{a}$ takes $2$ steps, while the parallel path $PC$ has $4$ steps. Their length ratio is $\\frac{4}{2}=2$. The remaining path points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$, so\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=-2\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\nAdding the path vectors gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}\\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{b}+(-2\\mathbf{a})\\\\\n&=-2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBecause $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are nonparallel, these coefficients are unique. Comparing with $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=-2$. The minus sign records the reversed direction." 13194139547592 17592